// `std.secret`: a secret and the theory that reads one by name. A `Secret.T`
// is sealed with no mark: `seal` puts text in, and no member reads it back.
// The checker refuses its crossing to another world, the printer spells it
// opaque at `Secret`, and no module of `std` compares two (#3375). Where the
// text comes from is a model of `Secrets`, which the user writes: a site
// names its key's secret, and the hosting process reads it through the
// model `gandr host --secrets` names, or from the environment variable
// `GANDR_SECRET_<NAME>` when it names none. A model's operation is pure, so
// a handler that decrypts runs before gandr does, and hands over its source.

/// A secret: text with a way in and no way out.
pub module Secret :> #{
  type T,
  seal : String => T
} {
  /// The text, inside the seal.
  type T = String;
  /// The text sealed.
  def seal(text : String) -> String { text }
}

/// Secrets by name: each name read as a secret of the sort `K`, which a
/// handler fixes at `Secret.T`.
pub theory Secrets {
  /// What a name reads as.
  sort K : Type+;
  /// The secret `name` names.
  oper secret : (name : String) --> K;
}
